%!testlib
%!
% The interpreter's private machinery dictionary is recorded in the
% context, where the collector roots it and the C reaches it. Two things
% hold of the dictionary recorded there, and the operator that records
% it is the only place either can be established.
%
% It is local. What it holds are local objects -- the device class
% dictionaries, the graphics scratch and template, the anchor procedures
% of the wrapped operators -- and a local object may not be stored in a
% global dictionary, so a global one is refused every write the
% machinery makes through it and the machinery quietly stops working.
%
% It is recorded once. The record is context state, not virtual memory:
% no `restore` puts back a dictionary displaced from it, so a second
% install stands for the rest of the run. Both are held in C, and the
% operator holding them is out of a program's reach entirely.


% --- and the operator that records it is not a program's to call ---
% Both properties above are established in C: the operator refuses a
% second install, and refuses a dictionary made where the machinery
% cannot write. Neither refusal is exercised from here, because the
% operator is not reachable from a program at all -- the lockdown leaves
% it in no dictionary, the way it already leaves the job store's
% accessor. That is the stronger property and it is the one asked for
% here. A program able to call it installs a private dictionary of its
% own, and the machinery reads that one for the rest of the job.
%
% internaldict is the root to ask, not systemdict: the password is
% published (PLRM 8), so a name moved into internaldict is as callable
% as one left in systemdict, and only absence from both is absence.
1183615869 internaldict /.setprivatedict known not
  (the operator recording the private dictionary is unreachable) assert
1183615869 internaldict /.setgraphicsdict known not
  (as is the one recording the graphics state) assert
1183615869 internaldict /.setjobstore known not
  (as is the one recording the job store) assert
1183615869 internaldict /.graphicsdictroot known not
  (and no operator answers with the graphics state by name) assert

% --- the refusal is of the install, not of every dictionary: the one
% the interpreter recorded at startup is still the one in hand ---
.privatedict type /dicttype eq
  (the interpreter still has its private dictionary) assert
% Asked of the wrapped procedures rather than the graphics state: the
% graphics state is rooted in the context now, so privatedict does not
% name it, and a marker that moved would say the dictionary was wrong.
.privatedict /.wrappedprocs known
  (it still holds the machinery it anchors) assert
.gscratch type /dicttype eq
  (the graphics scratch dictionary still answers) assert

% --- and what it holds is not a program's to replace ---
% A page-device request runs the maker it finds in there under the
% device's name, and that maker runs the class it finds under the
% class's name. A name here a program could write is a name that
% decides what runs as the device, so the dictionary takes no write
% from a program: not over a name the interpreter put there, not under
% a name of the program's own, and not away.
{ .privatedict /newPGMIMAGEdevice { } put }
  /invalidaccess (a device maker is not replaced) raises
{ .privatedict /.xpost_PGMIMAGE 3 dict put }
  /invalidaccess (nor the class the maker builds from) raises
{ .privatedict /newpngdevice { } put }
  /invalidaccess (nor is a driver's maker pre-empted before it loads) raises
{ .privatedict /.graphicsdict 3 dict put }
  /invalidaccess (nor the dictionary the graphics state lives in) raises
{ .privatedict /.a-name-of-my-own 1 put }
  /invalidaccess (nor may a name of a program's own arrive) raises
{ .privatedict /.graphicsdict undef }
  /invalidaccess (nor may a name be taken away) raises

% --- what the names answer with, and what those objects allow ---
% Sealing the dictionary settles which object each name answers with.
% What each object then allows is its own question, and the two answers
% differ: a device class is a declaration, settled once the boot has
% built it, and is closed -- a class left writable is a table a program
% can put its own method into and have the machinery run it. The scratch
% a render mutates is the opposite case and stays open, being where the
% machinery keeps what it writes.
.privatedict /.xpost_PGMIMAGE get wcheck not
  (a device class is closed once the interpreter is up) assert
.gscratch wcheck (and the scratch a render mutates is not) assert

% --- and a device is still built from it ---
{ << /OutputDevice /pgm /PageSize [ 40 40 ] >> setpagedevice }
  (a page-device request still builds the device it names) works

% --- and the machinery it roots still renders ---
{ newpath 100 100 moveto 200 100 lineto 200 200 lineto closepath
  0.5 setgray fill } (a page still paints through the recorded dictionary) works

% --- a restore does not hand the operator back either ---
% The record is context state and not virtual memory, so no restore puts
% back a dictionary displaced from it; that is held in C. What is asked
% here is the reachability question the same bracket would answer: the
% lockdown took the name out of internaldict, and a program must not get
% the operator returned to it merely by wrapping the attempt in save and
% restore. The verdict is carried out of the save on the operand stack,
% because a name defined inside it lives in local memory, which the
% restore reverts.
save
1183615869 internaldict /.setprivatedict known not
/verdict exch def
verdict exch restore
(the operator does not come back inside a save) assert
1183615869 internaldict /.setprivatedict known not
  (nor after the restore) assert

verdict
quit
